0. NOTICE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This image is the persisted state of one EXE-9 virtual processor core at the
  moment of its final halt.  It is not an archive and not a program container:
  it is a cold execution image.  The core is gone; the image is what remains.

  Sections 1-7 describe the machine as the core engineers left it, together
  with the encode-time records emitted when the image was written.  Section 8
  carries the operators' own final record.

1. IMAGE LAYOUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  All multi-byte fields are BIG-ENDIAN.

  HEADER (first 0x100 bytes of the file):
    offset  size  field
    0x0000  8     magic          = 45 58 45 39 43 4F 52 45  ("EXE9CORE")
    0x0008  2     version        = 0x0901
    0x000A  2     flags
    0x000C  4     segment count N
    0x0010  8     EXEC_SEED
    0x0018  8     TABLE_SEED
    0x0020  4     ENTRY          segment id of first executed CODE segment
    0x0024  4     (reserved, 0)
    0x0028  4     MANUAL         segment id of this primer
    0x002C  4     image size (total file length)
    0x0030  4     header CRC32   (zlib crc32 of bytes 0x0000..0x002F)
    0x0034  ..    reserved

  SEGMENT TABLE (N entries of 0x40 bytes, starting at 0x0100):
    +0x00  4  SEG_ID      unique id
    +0x04  4  TYPE        1=CODE 2=MANUAL 3=TBL 4=SEAL 5=GARB 6=TRC 7=SYM
    +0x08  8  OFFSET      absolute file offset
    +0x10  8  SIZE        bytes
    +0x18  4  FLAGS       bit0 = WRITABLE (stores allowed inside), else
                          stores fault WRITEPROT
    +0x1C  4  AUX         for CODE: dispatch bias (section 3)
    +0x20  4  CRC32       of the segment bytes as shipped
    +0x24 16  NAME        NUL-padded ASCII
    +0x34 12  reserved

2. CODE SEGMENTS - PAGING AND FETCH ROTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  2.1  A CODE segment is partitioned into 256-byte pages p = 0,1,2,...

  2.2  Page rotation chain (page bytes = the bytes AS STORED in the file):

      K   = 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15
      X   = 0xA5A5A5A5A5A5A5A5
      M   = 2^64

      m <- ROL64( (EXEC_SEED xor ((SEG_ID * K) mod M)) mod M , 19 )
      for p = 0,1,2,...:
          if p > 0:
              w    <- big-endian 64-bit word at page[p-1] offset 0 (8 bytes)
              m    <- ROL64( ((m xor w xor X) * K) mod M , 31 )
          ROT[p] <- m mod 64

  2.4  Encode-time trace excerpt - EXEC_MAIN (SEG_ID as in the table),
      first three pages as shipped:

          ROT[0] = 0x00000000
          ROT[1] = 0x00000039
          ROT[2] = 0x00000037

  2.5  Fetch at segment-relative pc (always a multiple of 8):

      raw  <- big-endian 64-bit word at SEG_OFFSET + pc, read from the
              segment's CURRENT memory contents (sections 3.4 stores are
              visible here); the read may cross a page boundary - use the
              rotation of page pc>>8.  Rotation chains are always computed
              from the bytes as shipped in the file.
      word <- ROL64(raw, ROT[pc >> 8])

      field extraction from `word`:
          bits 63..56   op_byte
          bits 55..48   rd
          bits 47..40   ra
          bits 39..32   rb
          bits 31..0    imm32

3. INSTRUCTION SET
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  3.1  Registers: R0..R15, 64-bit, initially zero, persistent across HALT
      links (section 4).  One flag: Z (set only by CMP).

  3.2  Rotating dispatch - decode index:

           idx = ( op_byte + ( (pc >> 4) + AUX ) ) mod 256

      AUX is this segment's table AUX field (mod 256).

  3.2b Encode-time decode record - EXEC_SELFTEST:
          stored op byte 0x00000093 at pc = 0x40, AUX = 0x47  ->  idx 0xE2  (LDRR)

  3.3  Dispatch table (idx -> mnemonic).  An idx not listed here decodes
      through the fallback table instead of faulting:

           PSH = [NOP, XOR, ADD, MOV, ROL, SHR, AND, SUB]   (idx mod 8)

      with register fields taken mod 16.  A desynchronised sweep therefore
      keeps executing plausible-looking arithmetic instead of announcing
      itself.

    03h  NOP
    0Ah  JNZ
    0Bh  LI
    12h  JMP
    16h  LUI
    1Dh  TRAP
    21h  ORI
    2Dh  MOV
    38h  ADD
    42h  ADDI
    4Ch  SUB
    55h  AND
    5Fh  OR
    6Ah  XOR
    73h  XORI
    7Eh  MUL
    87h  ROL
    8Fh  HALT
    91h  ROR
    9Ch  SHL
    A5h  SHR
    B0h  NOT
    BAh  LDW
    C3h  LDB
    C9h  ANDI
    CEh  STW
    D7h  STB
    E2h  LDRR
    ECh  CMP
    F5h  JZ

  3.4  Operand semantics (imm = imm32; `sext` = sign extension; `zext` =
      zero extension):

      NOP                 no operation
      LI   rd, imm        rd <- zext(imm)
      LUI  rd, imm        rd <- (zext(imm) << 32) mod 2^64
      ORI  rd, imm        rd <- rd or zext(imm)
      MOV  rd, ra         rd <- ra
      ADD/SUB/AND/OR/XOR rd, ra, rb      (mod 2^64)
      ADDI rd, ra, imm    rd <- (ra + sext(imm)) mod 2^64
      ANDI/XORI rd, ra, imm              (zext(imm))
      MUL  rd, ra, rb     rd <- (ra * rb) mod 2^64
      ROL/ROR rd, ra, imm rd <- ROTL/ROTR64(ra, imm mod 64)
      SHL/SHR rd, ra, imm (imm mod 64)
      NOT  rd, ra         rd <- ra xor 2^64-1
      LDW  rd, [ra+imm]   rd <- big-endian u64 at absolute address ra+sext(imm)
      LDB  rd, [ra+imm]   byte at absolute address ra+sext(imm)
      STW  [ra+imm], rd   store u64 - ONLY inside FLAGS.wr bit0 segments;
                           anywhere else: fault WRITEPROT.  A store changes
                           what fetch (2.5) and LDW read from that address.
      STB  [ra+imm], rd   store byte - same rule
      LDRR rd, [ra+imm]   rotated table read - section 5
      CMP  ra, rb         Z <- 1 if ra == rb else 0
      JZ/JNZ/JMP imm      pc <- pc + 8 + sext(imm)
      HALT imm            section 4
      TRAP imm            fault (intentional abort)

      Register operands rd/ra/rb greater than 15 are faults.

4. SEGMENT CHAINING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      On HALT:
          next_id <- (imm32 xor low32(R1)) mod 2^32
          if next_id == 0xFFFFFFFF: execution terminates (chain end)
          else: the segment with SEG_ID == next_id must exist and have
                TYPE = CODE; pc <- 0, registers persist, dispatch AUX is
                re-read from the new segment's table entry.
                Any other next_id is fault BADLINK.
      Links are data: a segment may be entered more than once.

5. TABLE SEGMENTS (TYPE = TBL)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  5.1  Data is organised as 64-byte rows; row index i covers absolute
      addresses SEG_OFFSET + i*64 .. +63.

  5.2  Row rotation chain (stored rows = as they appear in the file):

      rr[0] = (TABLE_SEED xor ((SEG_ID * 0x1BF5) mod 2^64)) mod 64
      rr[i] = ( rr[i-1]*17 + B + i*11 ) mod 64          for i >= 1

      where B = value of the FIRST BYTE of stored row i-1.

      stored row i  =  logical row i rotated LEFT by rr[i] bytes.

  5.2b Encode-time trace excerpt - segment DSEED, first four rows as
      shipped:

          rr[0] = 0x00000031
          rr[1] = 0x0000002D
          rr[2] = 0x00000030
          rr[3] = 0x00000016

  5.3  LDRR rd, [ra + imm]:
          phys <- ra + sext(imm)
          phys must lie inside a TYPE=TBL segment
          row_base <- SEG_OFFSET + ((phys - SEG_OFFSET) and NOT 63)
          k        <- phys - row_base           (must be <= 56)
          i        <- (row_base - SEG_OFFSET) / 64
          logical  <- stored_row_i rotated RIGHT by rr[i] bytes
          rd       <- big-endian u64 at logical[k..k+7]

6. EXECUTION CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  6.1  Start: segment id = HEADER ENTRY, pc = 0, all registers 0, Z = 0.
       Registers persist across every HALT link; the chain ends when some
       HALT yields next_id = FFFFFFFFh.

  6.2  The terminating segment leaves behind:

           R15 == 0x5354414C                      ('STAL' success marker)

       The state words for section 7 are R1..R4 as they stand at the
       terminating HALT.  No fold constants are embedded in the code.

  6.3  SPEC SELF-CHECK (commitment).  Execute the segment named
       EXEC_SELFTEST standalone (pc = 0, registers cleared) until its HALT.
       Compute SHA-256 over be64(R1) || be64(R2) || be64(R3) || be64(R4)
       of the end state.  It must equal:

           commitment = 0b6d8d4770eac443de6b9b73340a4a262117d9da42e6ced9411ebda286bbbcf2

7. OPENING THE WILL SEAL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  7.1  Key derivation from the final state:

           key = SHA-256( "LASTEXEC" || be64(R1) || be64(R2) || be64(R3)
                          || be64(R4) || "LASTEXEC" )

  7.2  SEAL-256 - 128-bit block, 256-bit key, 16 rounds.

       Key schedule: interpret key as four big-endian 64-bit words
       x0..x3.  For i = 1..17:
           for j = 0..3:
               t  <- ( x[j] + x[(j+1) mod 4]
                       + (0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 * (4*(i-1)+j+1)) mod 2^64 )
                     mod 2^64
               t  <- t xor ( x[(j+3) mod 4] >> 7 )
               x[j] <- ROL64(t, 13)
           RK[i-1] <- be64(x0 xor x2) || be64(x1 xor x3)     (16 bytes)
       yielding RK[0]..RK[16]  (17 round keys).

       Encryption of one 16-byte block st:
           for r = 0..15:
               st <- st xor RK[r]                (bytewise)
               st <- SBOX[st[t]] for each byte t
               st <- st[P[t]]   for each byte index t
           st <- st xor RK[16]

       Decryption:
           st <- st xor RK[16]
           for r = 15..0:
               st <- inverse permutation of P
               st <- INV_SBOX[st[t]]
               st <- st xor RK[r]

       Byte permutation P (forward: out[i] = in[P[i]]):
    00 05 0A 0F 04 09 0E 03 08 0D 02 07 0C 01 06 0B
       P inverse (out[i] = in[IP[i]]):
    00 0D 0A 07 04 01 0E 0B 08 05 02 0F 0C 09 06 03

       S-BOX (derived from the image, not printed here):
           1. read DLOOK (TYPE=TBL) logical rows 0..3 per 5.3 and
              concatenate them in order -> 256 bytes D;
           2. perm <- [0, 1, ..., 255];
           3. for i = 255 down to 1:
                  j <- D[i] mod (i + 1)
                  swap perm[i], perm[j]
           4. SBOX[x] = perm[x];  INV_SBOX is its inverse.

  7.3  Seal segment layout (TYPE = SEAL):

           [0 .. 15]      16 column-shift bytes, one per column
           [16 .. n-1]    ciphertext, L = (SIZE-16)/16 blocks of 16 bytes

       Column rotation applied to the ciphertext matrix (row-major, L rows
       of 16 bytes), inverse applied when opening:

           stored[r][c] = ct[(r - shift[c]) mod L][c]

       Open procedure:
           1. read shifts from bytes 0..15, L = (SIZE-16)/16
           2. undo the column rotation (move each column back up by shift[c])
           3. SEAL-256-decrypt every row under `key`
           4. concatenate, strip PKCS#7 padding (block size 16)
           5. the plaintext is whatever the key yields - this image ships no
              checksum for it; you decide what convinces you.

  7.4  The final execution's will is sealed in the segment named
       "WILL_SEAL".

